  "narrative": "The newly delivered validation responses materially change the current conclusions. First, H49 was already an accepted discovery association: TP53-loss lines had higher, less-negative dependency_USP7 scores than TP53-loss-negative lines, interpreted as weaker USP7 dependency. The independent validation estimate is very consistent with discovery: signed validation estimate +0.800 with independent interval +0.769 to +0.831, compared with discovery +0.802, +0.782 to +0.822. This effect is extremely large relative to any approximate 10% clinical-scale threshold for dependency_USP7 and the validation interval remains far from both null and threshold-scale null. I therefore accept H49 and close it as a supported same-dataset association, with no causal or external generalizability claim.\n\nSecond, H36, MYC amplification versus dependency_RPL11, was already rejected on discovery evidence because the registered stronger-dependency direction was not supported by the discovery interval. The independent validation does not rescue the claim: the signed validation estimate is small, +0.015, with independent interval -0.015 to +0.044. For the registered direction, this is not a clinically meaningful robust effect, and the validation is inconsistent with the discovery direction. I keep H36 rejected and closed as a negative/low-yield probe.\n\nThird, H90, the reversal of H84 for RB1 loss versus dependency_DHX9, was the key unresolved signal. H90 claimed that RB1-loss lines have weaker DHX9 dependency, i.e. higher and less-negative dependency_DHX9 scores. Discovery gave a suggestive positive signed estimate +0.0348, interval +0.0091 to +0.0605. The point estimate exceeded the approximate 10% DHX9 threshold (~0.025), but the lower bound was below threshold, motivating independent validation. The independent validation estimate is -0.0069, independent interval -0.0446 to +0.0309. Thus the validation point estimate is near zero and opposite to the claim, and the interval includes null, clinically meaningful positive effects, and clinically meaningful negative effects. Taken as a whole, the overall RB1-loss/DHX9 association is not supported as scientifically meaningful: discovery was borderline, the strict subset evidence was heterogeneous, and independent validation failed. I therefore reject H90 and close the investigation. The related same-dataset subset claims H91, H92, H93, H96, and H97 remain unresolved as exploratory subset estimates but are closed from active follow-up because they no longer provide a robust route to an accepted overall association.\n\nThe supported core same-dataset associations remain: SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high is associated with stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, with independent validation in the high-screen-quality subset; TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency, now independently validated; and CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency, independently validated. Negative or low-yield results include the overall RB1-DHX9 reversal, MYC-amplification versus dependency_RPL11, ARID1A-loss versus dependency_SMARCA2 in the registered direction, SMARCA4-loss versus dependency_POLR2A in the registered direction, and marker-D positive associations used as specificity controls. The NF1-loss versus BRAF claim is closed as unresolved low-yield after borderline discovery and non-supportive robustness checks.\n\nThe most informative next analysis is the already registered but untested PTEN-loss versus dependency_STAT3 comparison. STAT3 has had only weak or borderline probes so far, and PTEN loss provides a mechanistically distinct PI3K/AKT/mTOR-related perturbation that could test whether STAT3 dependency is context-dependent in this dataset. If PTEN loss yields a discovery effect clearly above the approximate STAT3 clinical scale and with interval bounds supportive of clinical significance, I would consider accepting it without validation or requesting validation if the interval remains near threshold. If it is null or inconsistent, I would close the STAT3 pathway family and return to broader coverage of less-tested predictors and outcomes. All conclusions remain limited to associations in this supplied lung-lineage CRISPR dataset, not causal mechanisms or generalizable biology.",
