  "narrative": "No new independent validation was delivered and no response deadline is due, so no claim-status change is required in this synthesis. The voluntary validation budget is exhausted; the accepted evidence state therefore reflects the discovery results in the supplied dataset plus the independent validations already completed earlier in the ledger. Direction convention remains important: more negative dependency scores mean stronger dependency. For direction -1, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency in the exposed group or a stronger subgroup contrast; for direction +1, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency in the exposed group. I interpret effects relative to the outcome scale and the supplied relative-significance criterion, while retaining the conservative practical view that very small absolute dependency-score differences are not scientifically decisive.   SUPPORTED DATASET-LEVEL ASSOCIATIONS: Six core associations are directionally coherent, scientifically meaningful in this lung-focused resource, and several have independent validation. First, SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency: H6/R6 signed estimate +0.784, CI +0.765 to +0.803; independent validation R144 +0.790, CI +0.760 to +0.820. This effect is enormous relative to the dependency-score scale and persists across many quality-control, library, lineage, expression, and co-alteration strata. Negative specificity checks support pattern specificity: ARID1A main effects on SMARCA2 were rejected (H252/R231, H253/R232), and SMARCA4 loss was rejected for DHX9 (H278/R253). Second, MSI-high status is associated with much stronger WRN dependency: H8/R8 +0.790, CI +0.760 to +0.819; validation R131 +0.799, CI +0.754 to +0.844. This is one of the largest and most robust effects in the dataset and survives multiple QC and context strata, including the high-mutation-burden check H285/R267 +0.780, CI +0.749 to +0.811. Low-mutation-burden restriction is structurally non-estimable rather than negative. Third, TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency: H49/R42 +0.802, CI +0.782 to +0.822; validation R52 +0.800, CI +0.769 to +0.831. The association is stable across Avana and non-Avana libraries, high Cas9 activity, adenocarcinoma, AXL-high, hypoxia-high, IFN-gamma-high, KEAP1-loss, and other strata. Interaction tests for lineage, MYC, STK11, KEAP1, IFN, and AXL did not support meaningful modification. Fourth, KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency: H17/R7 +0.796, CI +0.776 to +0.816; high-quality-screen stratum H24/R19 +0.790 was independently validated by R39 +0.799, CI +0.759 to +0.839. The association persists across Avana, non-Avana, high Cas9, adenocarcinoma, STK11-intact, AXL-high, hypoxia-high, IFN-gamma-high, and PTEN-loss contexts. A meaningful KEAP1 by STK11 interaction was rejected (H271/R258). Fifth, PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency: H103/R80 +0.267, CI +0.215 to +0.319; validation R92 +0.283, CI +0.208 to +0.358. This moderate effect is robust across serum, growth-rate, lineage, EMT, IFN, hypoxia, MYC-expression, and many co-alteration contexts. The accepted IFN-gamma-high interaction H156/R140 +0.212, CI +0.094 to +0.331, together with stratum contrasts H160/R145 +0.423 and H161/R146 +0.211, supports context enhancement in IFN-gamma-high lines, although the corresponding EMT interaction H179/R160 remains unresolved. Sixth, CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency: H59/R48 +0.117, CI +0.082 to +0.152; validation R65 +0.116, CI +0.065 to +0.166. This is the smallest core effect but is directionally stable and replicated across library, lineage, MTAP contexts, and other strata. New context checks strengthen but do not fully resolve regional-copy-number specificity: H288/R266 among MTAP-loss TP53-intact lines is +0.162, CI +0.070 to +0.255, and H67/R274 among Avana lines is +0.116, CI +0.079 to +0.153; however, the lower bounds are near or below the conservative 0.10-unit benchmark, and H289 cannot distinguish CDKN2A-specific from broader 9p21 effects because the interaction is structurally non-estimable.   NEGATIVE FINDINGS: The tested driver-self dependency hypotheses are largely negative. BRAF V600E does not support stronger BRAF dependency (H1/R1), KRAS mutation does not support stronger KRAS dependency (H2/R2), EGFR amplification does not support stronger EGFR dependency with validation R13, and ERBB2 amplification does not support stronger ERBB2 dependency (H4/R4). Co-driver-restricted retests for BRAF, KRAS, EGFR, and ERBB2 remained null or near null. Cross-pathway tests are also mostly negative: ERBB2 amplification versus STAT3 dependency was rejected with validation R26; MYC amplification versus RPL11 dependency was rejected with validation R79; MYC amplification versus STAT3 dependency was near null; and MYC amplification versus WRN dependency in MSS or high-Cas9 contexts did not replicate. The initial RB1-loss versus DHX9 association did not survive independent validation, so H84 and H90 are rejected. MTAP-loss strict MDM2 associations were rejected or ambiguous, including H162/R147 plus validation R157 and H178/H251/R230. ARID1A loss did not independently support SMARCA2 dependency within SMARCA4-loss or SMARCA4-intact contexts. APC mutation in high-WNT lines did not support TMED10 dependency. Most effect-modification interaction tests for the core associations were rejected or near null, indicating that the core effects are broadly stable rather than highly context-dependent within the tested strata.   UNRESOLVED UNCERTAINTY: Several questions remain open but do not overturn the core findings. The PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency EMT interaction H179 is suggestive but imprecise and should not be upgraded to a supported context effect without larger external data. The IFN-gamma-high CDKN2A-MDM2 main effect H262/R241 +0.080, CI -0.004 to +0.164, and hypoxia-high CDKN2A-MDM2 effect H248/R229 +0.095, CI +0.020 to +0.171, are compatible with a real effect but remain below the practical benchmark. The 9p21-region question for CDKN2A-MDM2 cannot be answered cleanly in this dataset because CDKN2A loss is structurally entangled with low 9p21 copy number, as shown by non-estimable H289/R273. H143/R126 +0.092 and H203/R177 +0.098 are unresolved small context estimates. Constructed marker D/E/F main effects and several low-priority STK11 or marker-specific contrasts remain unresolved or unestimated; these are not evidence of absence. All results are dataset-level associations in a supplied lung-focused resource, many lines being adenocarcinoma, with constructed assays and many correlated genomic variables. They are not causal, therapeutic, or externally generalizable claims.   MOST INFORMATIVE NEXT ANALYSIS: The best in-dataset discovery follow-up has already been taken as far as high-value Welch comparisons allow. The single most informative next analysis would be external replication of the six core associations, especially CDKN2A-loss MDM2 dependency and PTEN-loss STAT3 IFN-gamma interaction, in independent cell-line or perturbation datasets. If region-level copy-number data are available, a joint model of CDKN2A, MTAP, and 9p21 copy number is needed to determine whether the CDKN2A-MDM2 association is CDKN2A-specific or reflects broader 9p21 co-deletion biology. Larger external samples would also be needed to resolve the PTEN-loss STAT3 EMT interaction and to test whether modest IFN or hypoxia context effects are genuine. Additional discovery testing inside this dataset would mostly increase multiplicity without solving the remaining precision or structural identifiability problems."
}

